 "report": "Particle A42-spiral-e000-d000: inhibitory knot (k=5)\n\nWhat it is: a particle of mass 5.6 and radius 1.40 with 5 ports on its rim. Each port is a stick-slip contact with a dead zone (mean 0.38) and a 4-dim charge; 3 of its port pairs attract each other strongly (so copies can bind port to port). Inside, 10 couplings (80% inhibitory) pass a push from port to port; it settles in 3 sweeps with slip rate 1.00 and keeps its state (memory).\nHow it assembles (32 copies, 2 worlds): 0.81 bonds per particle, largest molecule 4.5 particles, 14.0 left free.\n\nIts matter vs an ESN of the same size (readout only, both with tuned input scale); controls: no bonds, random bonds:\n  memcap   0.117 vs ESN 0.997\u00b10.002  [below]  no bonds 0.114  random bonds 0.103\n  xor      0.603 vs ESN 0.534\u00b10.020  [beat]  no bonds 0.586  random bonds 0.599\n  narma10  0.354 vs ESN 0.839\u00b10.007  [below]  no bonds 0.310  random bonds 0.317\n  latch    0.858 vs ESN 0.677\u00b10.002  [beat]  no bonds 0.853  random bonds 0.862\n  mnist14  0.558 vs ESN 0.805\u00b10.018  [below]  no bonds 0.441  random bonds 0.547\n\nGood at: xor, latch.\nIts assembly does not matter: the particles do as well unbonded, so the useful part is the single particle's own stick-slip memory.\nUse (a guess): combining recent events nonlinearly (temporal feature detection); holding a value until it is overwritten, with nothing trained but the readout: a physical memory element for a recurrent or reservoir system."
